      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The pattern correlations between each model and ERSSTv5 (and the shared regression-pattern comparison) exist only over cells both fields cover on the common 1-degree grid, so one mask must be built and applied to all four fields.",
      "status": "summary_fallback",
      "fallback": "the notes failed the check",
      "violations": [
        "`here` is not in the selected fragments or the question"
      ],
      "rejected_notes": [
        "Each regression pattern comes from an analysis on its own source's cells: do not mask the patterns afterwards; correlate each with ERSSTv5 over the cells the pair shares.",
        "A cell-by-cell metric on one field instead needs one mask built from all sources and applied with `.where(mask)`; report the shared cell counts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The models provide top-cell bulk `tos`, not skin temperature, which must be stated when comparing the modelled PDO pattern and amplitude with an ERSSTv5 SST-based PDO.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`tos` is the bulk temperature of the top model cell; sea-ice-covered cells carry near-freezing under-ice water temperature and are not missing, so values near −1.8 °C are in the data — make ocean masks consistent before comparing.",
        "A model index is its anomaly from its own climatology, never from an observed one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The question prescribes bilinear regridding onto a 1-degree grid, so this fragment governs that regridding is acceptable for the pattern comparison but changes masks and is not conservative.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "For a grid-dependent analysis compared between sources (an EOF mode with its explained variance and regression pattern), put every source, observations included, on one common grid before the analysis and take its numbers there; quote the regridding method, since the pattern correlation depends on it.",
        "After regridding, build one shared valid-data mask for all sources before pattern statistics."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The 12-month lag autocorrelation and the Pearson correlations require effective sample sizes and the caveat that shared trends and autocorrelation inflate correlation, and the phase-transition/smoothed-index reasoning depends on it.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Comparing the rebuilt ERSSTv5 index with the published one: correlate as-is, no `detrend=True` — their shared trend is part of the agreement being checked, not a spurious link; with a small effective sample size the correlation is unstable.",
        "A pattern correlation across space has no effective-sample correction: read the coefficient, not its p-value."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "monthly-means-are-time-averages",
